Unfortunately, JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text-based data interchange format that represents structured data in an easy-to-read and human-friendly way. However, JSON doesn't support sorting of its key-value pairs as it is not a programming language. It's up to the developer's preferred programming language to implement sorting of JSON objects based on their keys.

That being said, if you're using JavaScript, you can use built-in methods like `Object.keys()`, `Array.from()`, `Array.sort()` and spread operator `...` to achieve this. Here's a simple implementation:

```js
function sortJsonByKeys(jsonObj) {
  const keys = Object.keys(jsonObj);
  return Object.fromEntries(
    Array.from(keys).sort((a, b) => {
      // define your sorting logic here
      // you can customize the comparison function based on your requirements
      // for example, to sort alphabetically, you can do:
      return a.localeCompare(b);
    }),
    value => [keys[value], jsonObj[keys[value]]]
  );
}

// usage:
const myJsonObj = { name: "John", age: 30, city: "New York" };
const sortedJsonObj = sortJsonByKeys(myJsonObj);
console.log(sortedJsonObj);
```

In this implementation, we first get all the keys from the JSON object using `Object.keys()`. Then, we convert the keys array into an iterable using `Array.from()` so that we can apply `sort()` method. Inside the `sort()` callback, we compare two keys using any desired sorting logic. Finally, we convert the sorted keys back into a JSON object using `Object.fromEntries()` and spread operator `...`. The resulting object will have the same values as the original JSON object, but with rearranged keys based on our sorting criteria.

