To make the circle element in your CSS look more 3D, you can apply some additional styles that create a sense of depth and perspective. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` style:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.8); /* new */
  transform: translateZ(10px); /* new */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

Here's how these new styles work:

1. `filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.8);` adds a drop shadow effect with some blurriness (the larger values for the horizontal and vertical offsets), as well as a lower brightness value to make the circle appear less bright and more recessed. This creates the illusion of a shadow being cast on the circle by an imaginary light source above and to the left.

2. `transform: translateZ(10px);` moves the circle forward by 10 pixels along the z-axis (which is perpendicular to the screen). This makes the circle appear to be farther away than before, which also contributes to the 3D effect.

You may need to experiment with different values for these styles to achieve the desired level of 3D-ness. Additionally, you may want to adjust the colors and gradients used in the circle to better suit your design.

Here's an implementation of the Fibonacci sequence generator using recursion in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <stdexcept>

// Function to generate Fibonacci sequence recursively
unsigned long long fibonacci(int n, int a, int b) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    else if (n == 1) return a;
    else return fibonacci(n - 1, b, a + b);
}

int main() {
    std::string input;
    int a, b, num;

    // Prompt user for first two elements
    std::cout << "Enter the first two elements of the Fibonacci sequence: ";
    getline(std::cin, input);
    std::stringstream ss(input);

    try {
        ss >> a >> b;
        if (ss.fail() || ss.peek() != ' ') throw std::invalid_argument("Invalid input");
        if (a < 0 || b < 0) throw std::out_of_range("First two elements cannot be negative");
    } catch (...) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Invalid input\n";
        return 1;
    }

    // Prompt user for number of sequence elements
    std::cout << "Enter the number of elements to generate: ";
    std::cin >> num;
    if (num <= 0 || std::cin.fail()) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Number of elements must be positive\n";
        return 1;
    }

    // Generate and print sequence
    std::cout << fibonacci(num, a, b) << ", ";
    for (int I = 1; I < num; i++) {
        std::cout << fibonacci(i, 0, 0) << ", ";
    }
    std::cout << fibonacci(num - 1, 0, 0) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

