response:
To implement bubble sort for sorting the given array of integers in ascending order with minimal memory usage and error handling, we can follow these steps:

1. Take the JSON input from the user or from a file using a JSON parsing library like nlohmann/json. We'll assume that the JSON is already parsed and available in the given structure.

2. Define a function `bubbleSort()` to perform bubble sort on the array. Here's the implementation:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int size) {
    bool swapped = true; // flag to keep track of whether any swap happened in a pass
    int end = size - 1; // set the last element as the end point of comparison

    while (swapped) { // keep running bubble sort until no more swaps occur
        swapped = false; // reset flag to false

        for (int I = 0; I < end; i++) { // iterate over all elements except the last one
            if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) { // check if the current element is greater than the next
                swap(arr[i], arr[i+1]); // swap the elements
                swapped = true; // set flag to true as a swap occurred
            }
        }
        --end; // move the end point of comparison one position left after each pass
    }
}
```

3. Call the `bubbleSort()` function inside the main function to sort the array.

```cpp
int main() {
    json j;
    // ... Parse JSON here ...

    int arr[] = j["array"];
    bubbleSort(arr, sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]));

    // ... Use sorted array here ...

    return 0;
}
```

